  "body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object is documented with the rule \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" I read this as: when a code fence contains at least one scoreable object, the returned object must be the last scoreable object inside that fence. The two reply texts are, in full, Reply A: \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1} {\\\"b\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\":3}\" and Reply B: \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1} {\\\"c\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\":3}\". For Reply A the last object inside the fence is {\\\"b\\\":2}, so the rule requires {\\\"b\\\":2}. For Reply B the last object inside the fence is {\\\"c\\\":2}, so the rule requires {\\\"c\\\":2}. The implementation, however, treats each fenced block as a single candidate; because the fenced content in each reply contains two objects, it fails to parse as one object, falls through to the top-level scan, and returns the last top-level object in the whole text, which is {\\\"z\\\":3} in both replies. Thus the check gives the same answer, {\\\"z\\\":3}, to both replies even though the rule requires different answers. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returned {\\\"b\\\":2} for Reply A and {\\\"c\\\":2} for Reply B, or if it returned any pair of different values for the two replies.",
  "commitments": "recover_json_object returns {'z':3} for both replies; the rule requires {'b':2} for Reply A and {'c':2} for Reply B; falsified if the function returns different values for the two replies."
